---
import Page from "../../layouts/Page.astro";
import Hero from "../../components/Hero.astro";
import Chapter from "../../components/Chapter.astro";
import Flow from "../../components/Flow.astro";
import NetworkDiagram from "../../components/NetworkDiagram.astro";
import Equation from "../../components/Equation.astro";
import CodeTabs from "../../components/CodeTabs.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import Compare from "../../components/Compare.astro";
import { MIGRATION, FME, REPORTING } from "../../data/flows.js";
import { MILEAGE_SQL, SQL_TABS, PY_TABS } from "../../data/code.js";
import { PERSON } from "../../data/site.js";

const N = 7;
const title = "Utility Network Engineering: Smallworld to Esri · Anshuman Choudhary";
const description = "Enterprise GIS case study: Smallworld to Esri migration, ArcGIS Utility Network concepts, PostgreSQL and PostGIS engineering, FME and Python automation, and Power BI reporting for electric utilities.";
const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org", "@type": "Article",
  headline: "Enterprise GIS and Utility Network engineering",
  description, author: { "@type": "Person", name: PERSON.name, url: "https://alrightansh.com/" },
  about: ["ArcGIS Utility Network", "Smallworld to Esri migration", "Electric utility GIS", "PostGIS", "FME", "ArcPy", "Power BI"],
};
---
<Page title={title} description={description} path="/work/utility-network/" crumb={{ href: "/work/", label: "Work" }} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <Hero a="Utility" b="networks" seed={23} pre="Case study · Enterprise GIS"
    lead="From legacy Smallworld data to a validated ArcGIS Utility Network, and the data engineering around it." />

  <section class="section" aria-label="Project overview">
    <div class="wrap">
      <dl class="overview" data-items="fade">
        <div><dt>Category</dt><dd>Enterprise electric utility GIS</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Status</dt><dd>Professional work, ongoing</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Role</dt><dd>Geospatial data scientist, Esri Platinum team at PwC</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Stack</dt><dd>ArcGIS Pro, ArcGIS Enterprise, Utility Network, PostgreSQL, PostGIS, FME, Python, ArcPy, Power BI</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <p class="note" data-block>Client names, systems, data and screenshots stay confidential. Every diagram and code sample on this page is
        illustrative and uses generic names.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Chapter id="migration" n={1} of={N} title="Smallworld to Esri" sub="A migration is a pipeline, not a one-off export" label="Migration">
    <p class="lead" data-block>Moving an electric utility from GE Smallworld to Esri is less about converting files than about carrying a
      network's behaviour across: what connects to what, what sits inside what, and which device feeds which customer.</p>
    <p class="body" data-block>I have delivered bulk utility migrations over multiple iterations. Python, FME and CI/CD turn each iteration
      into a repeatable run instead of a rebuild, so months of migration work become something that can be re-run, checked and compared.</p>
    <Flow id="migration-flow" steps={MIGRATION} mode="scroll" label="Smallworld to Esri migration lifecycle"
      kicker="Migration lifecycle · 8 stages" caption="The lifecycle in general terms. Each project tunes the stages to its data." />
  </Chapter>

  <Chapter id="utility-network" n={2} of={N} title="ArcGIS Utility Network" sub="How the network knows what it is" label="Utility Network">
    <p class="lead" data-block>The Utility Network models a grid as connected features with rules, not lines on a map. Domain networks hold
      the electric distribution and transmission systems, tiers split them into levels such as medium and low voltage, and rules decide what may
      connect to, sit inside or attach to what.</p>
    <NetworkDiagram id="un" />
    <div class="gloss">
      <div data-block>
        <h4>Structure</h4>
        <dl>
          <dt>Domain networks</dt><dd>Electric distribution and transmission each sit in a domain network, sharing one structure network of poles, vaults and ducts.</dd>
          <dt>Tiers</dt><dd>Levels such as medium and low voltage, each split into subnetworks. Electric networks usually use a partitioned tier definition; gas and water often use hierarchical tiers with tier groups.</dd>
          <dt>Network rules</dt><dd>Connectivity, containment and structural attachment rules list which asset types may connect, contain or attach. Anything else is an error on validation.</dd>
        </dl>
      </div>
      <div data-block data-delay="80">
        <h4>Subnetworks</h4>
        <dl>
          <dt>Subnetwork controllers</dt><dd>Sources such as a feeder's circuit breaker define where each subnetwork starts and which way it flows.</dd>
          <dt>Update Subnetwork</dt><dd>Stores each subnetwork's traced extent as a SubnetLine and records membership on its features.</dd>
          <dt>Dirty areas and validation</dt><dd>Edits leave dirty areas; validating the topology clears them and reports errors that block subnetwork updates.</dd>
        </dl>
      </div>
      <div data-block data-delay="160">
        <h4>Tracing and platform</h4>
        <dl>
          <dt>Network attributes</dt><dd>Values the network can read while tracing, such as whether a device is open or closed.</dd>
          <dt>Traces</dt><dd>Upstream, downstream, subnetwork, connected, isolation and shortest path, kept as named trace configurations.</dd>
          <dt>Enterprise geodatabase</dt><dd>Multi-user editing runs in an enterprise geodatabase with branch versioning, published through ArcGIS Enterprise as services.</dd>
        </dl>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Chapter>

  <Chapter id="network-data" n={3} of={N} title="Overhead and underground" sub="Mileage from network data" label="Network data">
    <p class="lead" data-block>How many miles of line run overhead, and how many underground, on each circuit? The answer comes from
      overlaying each subnetwork's extent with the electric lines, classing every line by its asset group, and measuring length in the database.</p>
    <Equation out="miles" terms={[
      { key: "len", show: "ST_Length(geom)", def: "Length of each line in its coordinate system's units: metres in a projected system. Check the spatial reference first; geographic coordinates need a geography cast or a projection." },
      { key: "km", show: "1000", op: "÷", def: "Metres to kilometres." },
      { key: "mi", show: "0.62", op: "×", def: "Kilometres to miles. The exact factor is 0.621371; 0.62 is the working approximation." },
    ]} />
    <ol class="steps" data-items>
      <li><h4><span>01</span>Classify</h4><p>Group lines into overhead, underground or other from their asset group, using the agreed business mapping.</p></li>
      <li><h4><span>02</span>Overlay</h4><p>Intersect each subnetwork's SubnetLine with the electric lines, so every metre is credited to its circuit.</p></li>
      <li><h4><span>03</span>Measure</h4><p>Sum the lengths per subnetwork and class in SQL, then convert to miles.</p></li>
      <li><h4><span>04</span>Validate</h4><p>Watch for double counting where geometries overlap or lines are split, and reconcile totals against the source attributes.</p></li>
    </ol>
    <CodeBlock code={MILEAGE_SQL} lang="sql" caption="Illustrative PostGIS · generic names" />
  </Chapter>

  <Chapter id="databases" n={4} of={N} title="Spatial databases" sub="PostgreSQL, PostGIS and the enterprise geodatabase" label="Spatial databases">
    <div class="split2">
      <div>
        <p class="lead" data-block>An enterprise geodatabase is still a PostgreSQL database underneath. Knowing both layers lets quality
          checks, overlays and reporting run as set-based SQL next to the data, while edits keep flowing through ArcGIS.</p>
        <ul class="chips" data-items="fade" aria-label="Capabilities">
          <li>Spatial SQL</li><li>Geometry operations</li><li>Spatial joins</li><li>Spatial indexing</li><li>Query plans</li>
          <li>Aggregation</li><li>Data quality checks</li><li>Feature class management</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="body" data-block>Branch versioning keeps every edit as a row in the same table. Reporting SQL has to resolve the current
          state of the default version, the latest row per feature that is not a delete, or read through a published service. Those
          versioning columns are geodatabase metadata, not asset attributes, and never belong in a report.</p>
        <p class="body" data-block>In a PostgreSQL geodatabase the shape column can be PostGIS geometry or Esri's ST_Geometry; with the
          latter, the sde.st_* functions stand in for the PostGIS ones used here.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <CodeTabs id="sql" tabs={SQL_TABS} caption="Illustrative PostGIS · generic names" label="SQL examples" />
  </Chapter>

  <Chapter id="etl" n={5} of={N} title="FME automation" sub="Enterprise GIS ETL that re-runs on demand" label="FME automation">
    <p class="lead" data-block>FME carries the heavy lifting of a migration: reading source formats, mapping attributes, reshaping geometry and
      writing to PostgreSQL or the geodatabase. SQL and Python fill the gaps that transformers alone handle poorly.</p>
    <Flow id="fme-flow" steps={FME} label="FME workflow from source data to PostgreSQL or Esri"
      caption="A typical workspace. Transformers shown are common choices, not the record of one project." />
    <div class="split2 block">
      <div data-block>
        <h3 class="h3">SQL where the data is</h3>
        <p class="body">SQLExecutor runs lookups, spatial joins and aggregates inside PostgreSQL, next to the indexes, instead of pulling every
          feature through the workspace.</p>
      </div>
      <div data-block data-delay="80">
        <h3 class="h3">Python where logic is</h3>
        <p class="body">PythonCaller and scripted runners cover custom parsing, API calls and anything easier to test as code. Scheduled, the
          workspaces become a pipeline that runs batch after batch, ZIP archives included.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Chapter>

  <Chapter id="python" n={6} of={N} title="Python and ArcPy" sub="Python-powered GIS engineering" label="Python and ArcPy">
    <p class="lead" data-block>Python ties the stack together: ArcPy for geodatabase and geoprocessing work, a SQL driver for PostGIS, and plain
      Python for batch runs, checks and reports.</p>
    <CodeTabs id="py" tabs={PY_TABS} caption="Demonstrations · placeholder paths" label="Python examples" />
  </Chapter>

  <Chapter id="reporting" n={7} of={N} title="Power BI" sub="From network data to operational insight" label="Reporting">
    <p class="lead" data-block>Reporting reads from PostgreSQL, not from map layers. SQL views shape the network into a reporting model, and
      Power BI turns it into dashboards that filter by circuit, voltage class and placement.</p>
    <Flow id="bi-flow" steps={REPORTING} label="Reporting architecture from enterprise GIS to Power BI" />
    <Compare caption="Import or DirectQuery" a="Import" b="DirectQuery" rows={[
      { k: "Freshness", a: "As of the last scheduled refresh", b: "Live at query time" },
      { k: "Speed", a: "Fast: the data sits in memory", b: "Bound by the database and the network" },
      { k: "Model size", a: "Limited by capacity: all rows are stored", b: "Nothing stored but the model" },
      { k: "Database load", a: "Only while refreshing", b: "Every visual interaction sends queries" },
      { k: "Modelling", a: "Full Power Query and DAX", b: "Some transformations and functions are limited" },
    ]} />
    <p class="body" data-block>Import suits mileage and asset summaries refreshed on a schedule; DirectQuery suits small, filtered views that
      must be live. Either way, geometry stays out of the model: lengths are measured in SQL and only the numbers travel.</p>
  </Chapter>

  <nav class="next wrap" aria-label="Next case study">
    <a href="/work/utilities-in-3d/">
      <span class="idx" data-split data-stagger="14">Next</span>
      <span class="next-title" data-split>Utilities in 3D <span class="arr">→</span></span>
    </a>
  </nav>
</Page>
